# Copy this file to .env and fill in your real values.
# NEVER commit the .env file.

# --- Image generation backend ---
# Which backend to use for image generation. Switch to match your spend/quota.
#   vertex  = Vertex AI (default; uses GCP project + ADC / gcloud auth)
#   gemini  = Gemini Developer API (uses GEMINI_API_KEY below)
# Overridable per CLI run (--provider) and per API job (provider form field).
IMAGE_PROVIDER=vertex

# Optional per-provider model overrides (both default to gemini-3.1-flash-image).
# VERTEX_IMAGE_MODEL=gemini-3.1-flash-image
# GEMINI_IMAGE_MODEL=gemini-3.1-flash-image

# Base seed for image generation. BEST-EFFORT, not a guarantee: the API accepts
# `seed` and it does change the picture, and back-to-back calls with one seed
# reproduced pixel-for-pixel — but the same test on panel requests did NOT
# reproduce. Treat it as "more stable than nothing", never as "the board will
# redraw identically".
# The effective seed is derived per request from the prompt, so different panels
# still differ. Retry/regenerate buttons and the reference endpoints deliberately
# send NO seed — that is how you ask for a different picture.
# Set to "none"/"off" to disable seeding entirely (every call varies, as before).
# IMAGE_SEED=42

# --- Text/LLM backend (Script → Storyboard shot breakdown) ---
# Independent switch, same values as IMAGE_PROVIDER: vertex (default) or gemini.
TEXT_PROVIDER=vertex
# Optional per-provider text-model overrides.
# NOTE: `gemini-2.5-flash` is a rolling ALIAS — the model behind it changes. Pin
# a dated snapshot here if you need breakdowns to stay comparable across weeks.
# THE TWO BACKENDS DEFAULT DIFFERENTLY, and that is deliberate: Vertex still
# serves gemini-2.5-flash, while a Developer API key minted today is REFUSED by
# it — "no longer available to new users … use models/gemini-3.6-flash" — which
# arrives as a 404 and reads like a typo. So gemini defaults to gemini-3.5-flash.
# VERTEX_TEXT_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash
# GEMINI_TEXT_MODEL=gemini-3.5-flash

# --- The ✨ AI Editor chat: its own provider, model and key -----------------
# Every capability in this app switches independently, and the chat has its own
# name. Each of these three falls back to the shared text setting when unset.
#   CHAT_PROVIDER   = vertex | gemini | stub  (default: its key, then TEXT_PROVIDER)
#   CHAT_MODEL      = a model id              (default: the text model)
#   GEMINI_KEY_CHAT = a Developer API key     (default: GEMINI_API_KEY)
#
# ⚠ THE KEY IS ALSO THE SWITCH. Setting GEMINI_KEY_CHAT moves the chat to the
# Developer API on its own — pasting a key that then does nothing until you
# remember a second line is the bug this avoids. CHAT_PROVIDER still wins, so
# CHAT_PROVIDER=vertex moves it back without deleting a key you want again.
#
# ⚠ WHY THE CHAT IS BILLED APART: a chat turn is a per-MESSAGE cost on somebody's
# subscription and a breakdown is a per-RENDER cost, and on one key the two bills
# cannot be told apart. It is insulation too — a GCP project whose billing lapses
# answers 403 CONSUMER_INVALID to every Vertex call, and one shared switch takes
# the whole app quiet at once instead of the workflow that actually lapsed.
# CHAT_PROVIDER=gemini
# CHAT_MODEL=gemini-3.5-flash
# GEMINI_KEY_CHAT=your_chat_gemini_api_key_here
#
# The media half of the same idea. ⚠ NOT READ BY ANYTHING YET — images
# (gemini_client.py) and video (video_client.py) still resolve their own
# provider, so setting this today changes nothing.
# GEMINI_KEY_MEDIA=your_media_gemini_api_key_here

# --- Breakdown sampling (determinism) ---
# The script breakdown is an extraction task, so it defaults to greedy decoding
# with a fixed seed: the same script gives the same shot list. Note that no
# Gemini endpoint guarantees bit-exact reproducibility — this makes runs
# comparable, not identical. Image generation has no seed parameter at all, so
# panels and character references vary run to run regardless of these.
# TEXT_TEMPERATURE=0.0     # raise (e.g. 0.4) for varied readings of one script
# TEXT_TOP_P=1.0
# TEXT_SEED=42             # "none"/"off" = let the backend pick (varies per run)

# --- 🎬 The Director (Make Video: the auto-editor's brain) ---
# Reads an animatic and writes an EDIT PLAN — where the dissolves go, which shots
# move, what goes on screen. Two TEXT calls per run and nothing else: it renders
# nothing, spends no image quota and never calls Veo. The plan comes back to the
# browser, is shown as a table, and does not touch the timeline until Run.
#
# Its own switch, like every other capability here. Unset, it follows
# TEXT_PROVIDER, so an existing .env needs no change at all.
#   vertex | gemini      — as everywhere else in this file
#   stub                 — answers from a JSON file instead of a model. Not a
#                          test artefact: it is how you drive the whole workflow
#                          (popup, preview, run, revert) with no credentials and
#                          no quota.
#   openai_compatible    — ⚠ THE ONE NON-GOOGLE PROVIDER IN THIS APP. See the
#                          block below before you use it.
# DIRECTOR_PROVIDER=vertex
# DIRECTOR_MODEL=gemini-2.5-flash    # unset = whatever the text model is
# DIRECTOR_STUB_PATH=director_stub.json   # only read when DIRECTOR_PROVIDER=stub
#
# --- Somebody else's model (added 2026-08-23, Phase 5) ---
# ⚠ NO SECOND SDK WAS ADDED FOR THIS, and none should be. `openai_compatible`
# is a WIRE FORMAT — one POST to {DIRECTOR_BASE_URL}/chat/completions over
# `requests`, which requirements.txt has always had. That one format reaches
# OpenAI, Ollama, vLLM, llama.cpp, LM Studio, Groq, Together and OpenRouter, and
# anything behind an OpenAI-shaped proxy. `openai` is accepted as an alias.
#
# ⚠ THE DIRECTOR IS THE ONLY CAPABILITY THIS APPLIES TO. Images, Veo, TTS and
# captions are Google-only and unaffected — the Director is the one thing built
# behind an adapter seam (`llm_json.complete_json`), which is what makes this a
# day's work instead of a rewrite.
#
#   # OpenAI
#   DIRECTOR_PROVIDER=openai_compatible
#   DIRECTOR_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1
#   DIRECTOR_MODEL=gpt-4.1
#   DIRECTOR_API_KEY=sk-...
#
#   # Ollama on this laptop — no key, no quota, no bill
#   DIRECTOR_PROVIDER=openai_compatible
#   DIRECTOR_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1
#   DIRECTOR_MODEL=qwen2.5:14b
#
# DIRECTOR_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1
# DIRECTOR_API_KEY=
# DIRECTOR_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=180   # one attempt's ceiling (BOTH providers now)
# DIRECTOR_THINKING_TOKENS=1024  # ⚠ the biggest latency knob there is — see below
# DIRECTOR_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=12288
# DIRECTOR_BUDGET_SECONDS=135    # ...and the wall clock for one call, retries included
#
# ⚠ DIRECTOR_THINKING_TOKENS IS WHAT MAKES 🎬 FAST OR SLOW. 2.5-class models
# think with an AUTOMATIC budget unless told otherwise, and that is where the time
# went: measured on gemini-2.5-flash, a 24-shot board took 133s automatic and 28s
# at 1024, and an 8-shot board once ran past the budget and came back 504.
# ⚠ DO NOT SET IT BELOW ~1024: at 512 the polish call ran out of room and returned
# a plan with no steps in it, and at 0 it loses the thread and generates until the
# output cap stops it (65,536 tokens / 238s with no cap). Higher buys time, not
# steps. -1 restores the provider's automatic budget.
#
# ⚠ THE PLAN ROUTE MAKES TWO CALLS, so the browser has to be willing to wait for
# 2 × DIRECTOR_BUDGET_SECONDS. It waits 300s (`PLAN_TIMEOUT_MS` in client/src/api.js).
# Raise the budget past ~145s and you must raise that too, or the tab aborts a
# request the server is still correctly serving — and the paid call is billed anyway.
#
# ⚠ DIRECTOR_MODEL IS REQUIRED HERE and there is no default. The fallback is the
# TEXT model's id, which is a Google one — sending it to someone else's endpoint
# buys a 404 after the request instead of a sentence before it.
#
# --- Where the schema travels ---
# Vertex and the Gemini API take a JSON Schema and enforce it server-side. Most
# other endpoints cannot, so the schema is written INTO the prompt as a shape
# sketch, and what comes back is extracted (fence, prose and all), parsed, and —
# if it will not parse — handed back to the model ONCE with the error, to mend.
#   auto    — native on vertex/gemini/stub, prompt everywhere else. The default.
#   native  — the provider enforces the schema.
#   prompt  — the schema goes in the message.
# ⚠ SET IT TO `prompt` ON GOOGLE TO REHEARSE THE OTHER PATH. It turns the model
# you already have keys for into a stand-in for one that cannot be handed a
# schema, which is the cheapest honest test of this whole feature.
# DIRECTOR_STRUCTURED_OUTPUT=auto
#
# `response_format: json_object` on an OpenAI-shaped call. It is NOT a schema —
# it only makes prose-around-the-JSON rarer — so the sketch still goes in the
# prompt either way. Turn it off if your endpoint rejects the field (the error
# message quotes the body, so you will see it).
# DIRECTOR_JSON_MODE=on
#
# --- Will model X work? ---
# ⚠ THERE IS A TEST THAT ANSWERS THAT, AND IT IS THE ONLY HONEST ANSWER:
#
#     DIRECTOR_CONTRACT_LIVE=1 python tests/director_contract_check.py
#
# It sends one committed six-shot board (tests/golden_brief.json) through
# whichever provider is configured above and asserts the four things that
# matter: a valid plan, every shot covered, no invented vocabulary, and the
# house guard rails respected without the fence having to trim. Two text calls,
# no images, no Veo. ⚠ Without DIRECTOR_CONTRACT_LIVE it runs against a recorded
# answer and calls nothing, because no test in this repo spends by default.
#
# Sampling. An edit plan is a CONSIDERED ANSWER, not a lottery ticket: the same
# board must give the same film twice, or the preview's "Read it again" stops
# being a comparison and becomes a re-roll. So it defaults to greedy decoding
# with a fixed seed, exactly as the script breakdown does — and with the same two
# caveats: no Gemini endpoint is bit-exact (this makes runs COMPARABLE, not
# identical), and `gemini-2.5-flash` is a rolling alias, so pin DIRECTOR_MODEL to
# a dated snapshot if you need plans comparable across weeks.
# DIRECTOR_TEMPERATURE=0.0   # raise it if you deliberately want a different cut
# DIRECTOR_TOP_P=1.0
# DIRECTOR_SEED=42           # "none"/"off" = a different reading every run

# --- Video backend (Animatics → Final Video, Veo) ---
# READ THIS BEFORE TURNING IT ON: video is the only thing in this app billed per
# second of output. An 8s clip runs roughly $0.24 (lite/720p) to $3+ (standard/
# 1080p with sound), and a 20-shot project is 20 clips. The UI estimates every
# render before it spends, but the quota and the bill are yours.
#
# There is NO Google Flow API. Flow is a Labs web app; its credits are a
# separate ledger and a Google AI Pro/Ultra subscription grants no API access at
# all. This calls Veo directly — the same model Flow runs on. See video_client.py.
#
# Same two values as IMAGE_PROVIDER, and an independent switch:
#   vertex — application-default credentials + a BILLING-ENABLED project (prod)
#   gemini — GEMINI_API_KEY, billed separately from any subscription (dev)
VIDEO_PROVIDER=vertex
# Vertex needs a REGION for Veo — "global" (which the image models require) does
# not serve it. us-central1 is the safe default.
# GOOGLE_CLOUD_VIDEO_LOCATION=us-central1
# Model per provider. Unset picks by the project's quality tier
# (lite/fast/standard); set these to pin an exact model you have access to.
# THE TWO BACKENDS NAME THE SAME MODEL DIFFERENTLY — don't copy one to the other:
#   Vertex AI            veo-3.1-generate-001     / -fast- / -lite-
#   Gemini Developer API veo-3.1-generate-preview / -fast- / -lite-
# A name from the wrong backend 404s with "not found or your project does not
# have access to it", which reads like an IAM problem but is just a typo. The
# workflow's setup banner lists the ids your project can actually use.
# VERTEX_VIDEO_MODEL=veo-3.1-fast-generate-001
# GEMINI_VIDEO_MODEL=veo-3.1-fast-generate-preview
# A render takes 1–3 minutes; these bound the wait and the in-flight count.
# VIDEO_POLL_TIMEOUT=900       # give up on one clip after this many seconds
# VIDEO_POLL_INTERVAL=10       # seconds between "is it done yet" checks
# VIDEO_MAX_CONCURRENCY=2      # simultaneous renders — Veo's quota is tight
# --- Spend guards (server-side; see server/config.py) ---
# API_MAX_VIDEO_SHOTS=60       # shots per project
# API_MAX_VIDEO_BATCH=12       # shots one "Render all" may submit at once.
#                             # ⚠ SINCE PHASE 4 IT ALSO DECIDES HOW MANY PASSES
#                             # a 🎬 Veo run is split into, and it is the ONLY
#                             # place the run can honestly be stopped. It is
#                             # served to the browser on GET /director/config
#                             # so no client ever hard-codes it.
# API_MAX_VIDEO_WORKERS=2      # render jobs in parallel, own pool

# Google Cloud — used by the google-genai SDK for Vertex AI (IMAGE_PROVIDER=vertex)
GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT=project-cf56be07-4f9e-45d4-9f4
GOOGLE_CLOUD_LOCATION=global

# Gemini Developer API key — required when IMAGE_PROVIDER=gemini
GEMINI_API_KEY=your_gemini_api_key_here

# Meshy.ai — for 3D model generation (Phase 1 CLI uses this; Phase 2/3 uses user's own key)
MESHY_API_KEY=your_meshy_api_key_here

# --- API (Phase 2 FastAPI backend) ---
# Job store backend: "firestore" (default, persistent) or "memory" (dev only).
API_JOB_STORE=firestore
# Firestore collection used for job records.
API_FIRESTORE_COLLECTION=character_jobs
# When API_JOB_STORE=memory, jobs are mirrored to this JSON file so a backend
# restart (e.g. uvicorn --reload) doesn't lose saved storyboards. Empty = pure RAM.
API_LOCAL_JOBS_PATH=.local_jobs.json
# Where uploaded reference images are stored before a job runs.
API_UPLOAD_DIR=uploads
# Concurrent pipeline jobs (keep small — each makes several Gemini calls).
API_MAX_WORKERS=2

# --- Image-API throttling (applies to EVERY image call in the process) ---
# One shared governor in gemini_client.py, so background jobs AND the synchronous
# cast/props/retry endpoints are bounded together. Tune from your real Vertex
# quota: Console → IAM & Admin → Quotas → "Generate content requests per minute".
# Max simultaneous in-flight image requests. Lower = gentler on a small quota.
IMAGE_MAX_CONCURRENCY=3
# Requests per minute (token bucket). Set 0 to disable the rate limit.
IMAGE_RPM=60
# How many storyboard panels to draw at once (the throttle above is the real cap).
STORYBOARD_PANEL_CONCURRENCY=2
# Attempts per image call. 429/quota errors use a long, patient backoff (they
# refill per-minute); other transient errors retry quickly. Raise if your quota
# is tight and boards still come back with failed panels.
IMAGE_MAX_RETRIES=5

# --- Auth (JWT login + MongoDB user store) ---
# Secret used to sign JWTs. REQUIRED in production — generate a long random string, e.g.:
#   python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(48))"
JWT_SECRET=
# JWT signing algorithm and access-token lifetime (minutes; default 1440 = 24h).
JWT_ALGORITHM=HS256
ACCESS_TOKEN_EXPIRE_MINUTES=1440
# User accounts store: "mongo" (default, MongoDB) or "local" (JSON file on disk,
# for dev when MongoDB is unreachable). Local store path is configurable.
API_USER_STORE=mongo
API_LOCAL_USERS_PATH=.local_users.json
# MongoDB connection for user accounts (used when API_USER_STORE=mongo).
MONGODB_URI=mongodb://localhost:27017
MONGODB_DB=character_api
API_USERS_COLLECTION=users

# --- Plan & Script: YouTube channel research (OPTIONAL) ---
# The agent reads the user's channel either way:
#   without this key -> Gemini opens the channel PAGE and reads it (name, what
#     the channel posts, recent video titles). Uses the Vertex/Gemini
#     credentials above; nothing extra to set up.
#   with this key    -> the YouTube Data API also gives EXACT subscriber and
#     view counts, measured publishing rhythm and best-performing videos.
# Either way it never invents numbers: a page read is told to state no counts.
# Get one: Google Cloud console -> enable "YouTube Data API v3" -> create an API
# key. Free quota is 10,000 units/day; a full research pass costs ~103 units.
# YOUTUBE_API_KEY=your_youtube_data_api_v3_key

# --- Job store (the system of record) ---
# Where the RECORD of everything you make lives: character runs, storyboards,
# animatics, and every workflow added later. Image/video BYTES are NOT stored
# here — they live on disk (or GCS), and their URLs are saved in the job record.
#   mongo      (default) MongoDB. Use this.
#   firestore  legacy, for existing GCP deployments.
#   memory     in-process + JSON mirror. Dev only, single process, easily lost.
API_JOB_STORE=mongo
API_JOBS_COLLECTION=jobs
# Migrate jobs from a previous memory/local run:  python migrate_jobs_to_mongo.py

# --- Script drafts (autosave) ---
# The script being typed in the text panel is saved as you write, so a refresh
# can't lose it. ONE draft per user; saving overwrites it. Stored in the SAME
# backend as accounts (API_USER_STORE above) — deliberately no separate switch,
# so accounts and their drafts can never end up in different places.
# API_DRAFTS_COLLECTION=script_drafts
# API_LOCAL_DRAFTS_PATH=.local_drafts.json
# API_MAX_SCRIPT_CHARS=400000

# --- Admin panel ---
# WHO CAN OPEN /admin. The role normally lives on the user document
# (`account_role: "admin"`), granted from the panel itself or from the CLI:
#
#     python seed_admin.py --email you@example.com --role admin
#
# ADMIN_EMAILS is the BOOTSTRAP and the way back in. Granting the first role
# needs an admin and there isn't one on a fresh database — and if the last admin
# is demoted by mistake there is no other door. Any address listed here is an
# administrator whatever its document says, so it is a FLOOR the panel cannot
# lower: the role control refuses (409) rather than pretending to work.
# ⚠ Comma-separated, no spaces needed. Leave empty in production once a real
# admin account exists.
# ADMIN_EMAILS=you@example.com,cofounder@example.com

# The activity log — registrations, sign-ins, failed sign-ins, and every admin
# action with who performed it. Stored in the SAME backend as accounts
# (API_USER_STORE above), like drafts, so there is one switch and no way to end
# up with accounts in one place and their history in another.
# API_EVENTS_COLLECTION=events
# API_LOCAL_EVENTS_PATH=.local_events.json
# How long an event is kept, in days. Enforced by a MongoDB TTL index, so expiry
# happens in the database and there is no cleanup job to run. 0 keeps for ever.
# API_EVENT_RETENTION_DAYS=180
# The local JSON mirror is rewritten whole on every append, so it is capped
# rather than allowed to grow without bound. Dev only.
# API_MAX_LOCAL_EVENTS=5000
# Ceiling on one page of the admin user/event tables. A guard against an
# accidental ?limit=100000 against a remote database, not a product decision.
# API_ADMIN_MAX_PAGE=200

# --- Feature flags (Admin → Features) ---
# What each account may SEE and USE. The registry is edited from the admin
# panel, not from here — these are only where it is stored and how long it is
# cached. Stored in the SAME backend as accounts (API_USER_STORE above).
#
# ⚠ EVERYTHING SHIPS ON. A deployment that never opens the Features screen
# behaves exactly as it did before flags existed; the guards are no-ops until
# something is deliberately switched off.
# API_FEATURES_COLLECTION=features
# API_LOCAL_FEATURES_PATH=.local_features.json
# How long a worker may serve a cached copy of the registry, in seconds. Read on
# nearly every request, changed a few times a week — so it is cached, and an
# admin's write bumps the cache in THAT process immediately. This number is
# therefore how long OTHER workers can lag behind a flag change; it only matters
# if you run more than one. 0 disables the cache (the test suite does this).
# API_FEATURE_CACHE_TTL_S=20

# --- Billing tiers (Admin → Pricing) ---
# ⚠ "TIERS", NEVER "PLANS": /plans is Plan & Script, the content-planning
# workflow, and has been since long before there was any billing here.
#
# Prices are edited in the admin panel, not here. ⚠ THEY ARE STORED AS INTEGER
# MINOR UNITS — 2800 is $28.00 — so nothing in the money path is ever a float.
# This says which currency those integers are in; it converts nothing.
# API_BILLING_CURRENCY=USD
# API_TIERS_COLLECTION=tiers
# API_LOCAL_TIERS_PATH=.local_tiers.json
# API_TIER_CACHE_TTL_S=60
#
# Discounts and the record of who is paying. A SALE has no code and applies
# automatically; a COUPON applies to nobody until it is typed. Both are created
# in Admin → Sales.
# API_OFFERS_COLLECTION=offers
# API_LOCAL_OFFERS_PATH=.local_offers.json
# API_SUBSCRIPTIONS_COLLECTION=subscriptions
# API_LOCAL_SUBSCRIPTIONS_PATH=.local_subscriptions.json
# API_OFFER_CACHE_TTL_S=30
#
# --- Usage counters (Phase 5) ---
# What each account has used this calendar month, so a tier's `limits` can be
# enforced. ⚠ DELIBERATELY NOT CACHED — a counter read from a cache is a counter
# that lets somebody over their limit for the length of the TTL.
# API_USAGE_COLLECTION=usage_counters
# API_LOCAL_USAGE_PATH=.local_usage.json
#
# ⚠ WHAT IS AND ISN'T ENFORCED. `projects` and `image_generations` accumulate
# over the month and are refused when full. `shots_per_project` and
# `story_pages` describe ONE request and are checked against it. `watermark` and
# `commercial_use` are NOT enforced by any guard and are labelled as such in the
# panel — a watermark is a change to what the exporter draws, and commercial use
# is a licence term no code can police.
#
# ⚠ THE OFFER STORE IS THE ONE THAT FAILS CLOSED. Features and tiers serve a
# stale copy when the database is unreachable, because a blank app is worse. An
# offer that cannot be read is a discount nobody is currently entitled to, and
# inventing one gives money away — so no offers is the safe answer there.
#
# ⚠ NO PAYMENT PROVIDER IS CONNECTED. A tier records what an account is ON;
# taking money is Phase 4/6 of the admin panel plan. Moving somebody onto a paid
# tier from the panel charges nothing.

# --- Sound library (Freesound) ------------------------------------------------
# Switches the editor's "Sounds" tab on. With no key the tab is not drawn at all
# and nothing else changes, so this is optional.
#
#   1. Sign in (or sign up) at https://freesound.org/
#   2. Apply for a key at https://freesound.org/apiv2/apply/ — you describe the
#      app and it is issued immediately. Copy the "Client secret / Api key".
#   3. Put it below. ⚠ SERVER-SIDE ONLY. It must never be given a VITE_ prefix:
#      anything VITE_ is compiled INTO the browser bundle, and §B.4 of the
#      Freesound API terms requires this token to stay secret.
# FREESOUND_API_KEY=
#
# ⚠ A FREE KEY IS A NON-COMMERCIAL KEY. §3 of https://freesound.org/help/tos_api/
# reads: "Terms for commercial use of the API will be negotiated on a case by
# case basis with UPF." Before this ships in a product anyone pays for, email
# mtg@upf.edu, describe the app and the expected call volume, and get terms in
# writing. No environment variable can substitute for that — see the note at the
# top of `freesound.py`.
#
# ⚠ AND THE RATE LIMIT IS PER KEY, NOT PER USER: 60 requests a minute and 2000 a
# day on a free key, shared by everybody on the deployment. Ask UPF for a higher
# limit at the same time as the commercial terms.
#
# ⚠ THE SOUNDS THEMSELVES CARRY THEIR OWN, SEPARATE LICENCE. The app only ever
# lists CC0 and CC BY sounds — NonCommercial is fenced out on the server — and a
# CC BY sound has to be credited in the finished video. That credit is saved on
# the media-library card (`AnimaticAsset.attribution`) so it survives the edit.
